preConditionHook:
// A NAMED BROOKS SETUP, carried whole per the composite-carrying rule for named
// setups (the Harris patterns are the precedent): bars 2 and 0 form a TWO-BAR
// REVERSAL, skipping bar 1, and the skipped middle bar is SMALL relative to the
// recent average bar. The middle bar is what makes it a three-bar pattern rather
// than a gap in the data -- Brooks reads the small bar as the pause that lets
// the reversal count.
//
// THE TWO-BAR REVERSAL TEST IS THE CORPUS'S, TwoBarReversal's, not the merged
// slice's: a bear trend bar followed by a bull bar closing above the bear bar's RANGE
// midpoint (high + low)/2 whose own range is at least sizeRatio times the bear
// bar's. The slice's parameters (overlapFraction, bodySizeRatioMin/Max) belong
// to the overlap reading of the two-bar reversal, which the corpus examined and
// did NOT carry when TwoBarReversal was authored; using them here would embed a
// DIFFERENT two-bar reversal than the rule of that name, which is worse than
// losing three inputs. They are therefore not inputs.
//
// Not HarrisThreeBarPattern, which is pure high/low ordering across three bars
// with no body, midpoint or size term. Not TwoBarReversal itself, which reads
// bars 1 and 0 adjacent: a bar sits BETWEEN the pair here, so the two fire on
// different bars and neither implies the other.
//
// THE ENTRY ORDER IS NOT MECHANIZED. The source's buy stop one tick above the
// three-bar combined extreme collapses under the standing signal-bar-close /
// next-bar-open convention -- and a tick offset has no expression anyway, since
// the engine exposes no tick size. The rule is the pattern; the fill is the
// engine's.
//
// The average bar range mirrors the twin's average of the bar
// range over rangeLookback bars: an average over a PRICE expression, which has real history, so a plain loop
// over high[i]/low[i] is right and no zero-filled variable mirror is needed.
// The window is bars 0..rangeLookback-1, EL's own inclusive convention. The
// divisor 2 is a constant, never zero, so no guard against EasyLanguage's
// runtime divide-by-zero is needed.
//
// rngSum is a localVariables entry and is reset at the top of the hook rather
// than declared here, so the hooks survive a double placement.
//
// Deepest bar touched: max(2, rangeLookback - 1).
rngSum = 0.0;
for (kk = 0; kk < (int)rangeLookback; ++kk) {
    rngSum += high[kk] - low[kk];
}
avgRng = rngSum / rangeLookback;
